#. Source: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:224
#. URL: http://smart.who.int/trust-phw/business-processes.html
#: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:224
msgid "Why only one table. Each row is decided by  for that policy alone (, with the PCF Consent path of ), and applicability is a check of the ODRL target, purpose and assignee. Neither is a decision table, so there is no per-row DMN table. The DMN decision combines the rows once per request."
msgstr ""

#. Source: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:222
#. URL: http://smart.who.int/trust-phw/business-processes.html
#: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:222
msgid "Why PRIORITY. The owner's order (any deny, then any unknown, then any permit, then none applicable) is a ranking of outcomes, and PRIORITY is the DMN hit policy that returns the matching rule whose output comes first in the output's ordered . Each rule then states one condition, and the ranking resolves overlaps; for example, a request with a deny and an unknown matches rules 1 and 3, and  wins. Rules with the same output never overlap (rules 1 and 2 differ in ), so the result does not depend on rule order. COLLECT was not used: its aggregators (, , , ) work on numbers, so the outcomes would have to be coded as numbers that no standard defines. FIRST would also work, but it hides the ranking in the rule order. Every combination of up to three policies, with every outcome and strategy, was checked against the owner's rule, and exactly one outcome results each time."
msgstr ""

#. Source: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:217
#. URL: http://smart.who.int/trust-phw/business-processes.html
#: input/pagecontent/business-processes.md:217
msgid "Wins only when rules 1 to 3 do not match."
msgstr ""

